| # A single quote is not shell syntax the tokenizer refuses — it strips the
| # quotes and reads what is inside as one word, the way the shell would. So
| # `'-c'` is the flag `-c`, and the launch stays the direct one GNU Make would
| # have made: it assembles `/bin/sh '-c' "echo one > out"`, re-tokenizes, and
| # execs `/bin/sh` `-c` `echo one > out` with no shell in between.
| #
| # THE GATE IS THE FILE `out`. Splitting `.SHELLFLAGS` on whitespace alone would
| # have kept the quote bytes and passed `'-c'` as an argument `/bin/sh` reads as
| # a file name to open, so the recipe would not run. Tokenizing the flags the
| # way the assembled line is tokenized keeps the quotes off and the recipe runs.
| SHELL := /bin/sh
| .SHELLFLAGS := '-c'
| all:
| 	@echo one > out
